{"cuisine/native-american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/native-american-cuisine","n":"Native American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Indigenous cuisine of the Americas includes all cuisines and food practices of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas. Contemporary native peoples retain a varied culture of traditional foods, along with the addition of some post-contact foods that have become customary and even iconic of present-day Indigenous American social gatherings (e.g. frybread). Foods like cornbread, turkey, cranberry, blueberry, hominy, and mush have been adopted into the cuisine of the broader United States population from Native American cultures."],["In other cases, documents from the early periods of Indigenous American contact with European, African, and Asian peoples have allowed the recovery and revitalization of Indigenous food practices that had formerly passed out of popularity."],["The most important Indigenous American crops have generally included Indian corn (or maize, from the Taíno name for the plant), beans, squash, pumpkins, sunflowers, wild rice, sweet potatoes, tomatoes, peppers, peanuts, avocados, papayas, potatoes and chocolate."],["Indigenous cuisine of the Americas uses domesticated and wild native ingredients. As the Americas cover a large range of biomes, and there are more than 574 currently federally recognized Native American tribes in the US alone, Indigenous cuisine can vary significantly by region and culture. For example, North American Native cuisine differs from Southwestern and Mexican cuisine in its simplicity and directness of flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"native-american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Sioux Chef's Indigenous Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sean Sherman with Beth Dooley, 2017","note":"Pre-colonial Indigenous ingredients and dishes of the Dakota and Minnesota territory, from the Oglala Lakota chef.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780816699797","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780816699797"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Bannock (Indigenous American food)","dish/bannock-indigenous-american-food"],["Frybread","dish/frybread"],["Grits","dish/grits"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Native American cooking","count":31,"links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"],["Amazonian Kichwa foodways","cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways"],["Anishinaabe foodways","cuisine/anishinaabe-foodways"],["Aymara foodways","cuisine/aymara-foodways"],["California Indigenous foodways","cuisine/california-indigenous-foodways"],["Cherokee foodways","cuisine/cherokee-foodways"],["Coast Salish foodways","cuisine/coast-salish-foodways"],["Diné foodways","cuisine/dine-foodways"],["Eeyou Cree foodways","cuisine/eeyou-cree-foodways"],["Garifuna foodways","cuisine/garifuna-foodways"],["Guaraní foodways","cuisine/guarani-foodways"],["Guna foodways","cuisine/guna-foodways"],["Haudenosaunee foodways","cuisine/haudenosaunee-foodways"],["Inuit cuisine","cuisine/inuit-diet"],["Lakota foodways","cuisine/lakota-foodways"],["Mapuche foodways","cuisine/mapuche-foodways"],["Maya cuisine","cuisine/maya-cuisine"],["Métis foodways","cuisine/metis-foodways"],["Mixtec foodways","cuisine/mixtec-foodways"],["Mvskoke foodways","cuisine/mvskoke-foodways"],["Nahua foodways","cuisine/nahua-foodways"],["Nuu-chah-nulth foodways","cuisine/nuu-chah-nulth-foodways"],["Pueblo foodways","cuisine/pueblo-foodways"],["Purépecha foodways","cuisine/purepecha-foodways"],["Quechua foodways","cuisine/quechua-foodways"],["Rarámuri foodways","cuisine/raramuri-foodways"],["Taíno heritage foodways","cuisine/taino-heritage-foodways"],["Tohono O'odham foodways","cuisine/tohono-o-odham-foodways"],["Wampanoag foodways","cuisine/wampanoag-foodways"],["Wayuu foodways","cuisine/wayuu-foodways"],["Zapotec foodways","cuisine/zapotec-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6e/Manoomin_%28wild_rice%29.jpg/500px-Manoomin_%28wild_rice%29.jpg","w":500,"h":669,"by":"Jacob d'UPtownr","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manoomin_(wild_rice).jpg"}},"cuisine/galician-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/galician-cuisine","n":"Galician cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["Galician cuisine refers to the typical dishes and ingredients found in the cuisine of the autonomous community of Galicia, Spain. These include shellfish, empanadas, polbo á feira (a dish made of octopus), cheese queixo de tetilla, ribeiro and albariño wines, and orujo liquor. Similarly to Asturian cuisine, Galician dishes have maintained several Celtic links, namely with different stews."],["The potato is a staple food in the region, first arriving in Spain from the Americas in the 16th century, and then grown first and foremost on the coasts of the Ría de Noia. In Galician cuisine, neither the cook nor the recipe really matters; what is being served is the central part of the cuisine."],["In Galicia, a wide variety of sea produce can be found in traditional dishes, due to the province's long shoreline and traditional fishing economy. Agricultural products such as potatoes, maize, and wheat are also staples in the Galician diet, along with dairy and meat products from cattle, sheep, and pigs; Galicia's grasses are green year-round and are excellent for grazing."],["Due to the history of a weak economy, little industry, and overall a less-than-prominent position in Spanish politics and culture, the development of a Galician haute cuisine has been slowed until recently, with chefs such as Toñi Vicente gaining national attention only since the 1980s."],["Galician bread (pan galego) is appreciated in the autonomous community and throughout Spain for its unique quality. It is a high-hydration bread whose flour is a mixture of indigenous and foreign wheats. In 2019 Galician bread was protected with the Protected Geographical Indication (PGI) certificate of the European Union."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"galician-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galician_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Androlla","dish/androlla"],["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Lacón","dish/lacon"],["Polbo á feira","dish/polbo-a-feira"],["Tarta de Santiago","dish/tarta-de-santiago"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chorizo","ingredient/chorizo"],["Lacón","ingredient/lacon"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Pig fat","ingredient/pig-fat"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rapini","ingredient/rapini"],["White bean","ingredient/white-bean"],["Boston butt","ingredient/boston-butt"],["Dried meat","ingredient/dried-meat"],["Common octopus","ingredient/common-octopus"],["Octopus as food","ingredient/octopus-as-food"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Almond meal","ingredient/almond-meal"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cinnamomum verum","ingredient/cinnamomum-verum"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Sherry","ingredient/sherry"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/03/Centola_%28128473968%29.jpg/500px-Centola_%28128473968%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"eu -","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centola_(128473968).jpg"}},"dish/apple-bread":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/apple-bread","n":"Apple bread","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Apple bread is a popular vegetarian snack in Taiwan, commonly enjoyed as a breakfast item or a light snack. The recipe was created by Liu Zheji (Chinese: 劉哲基) and is widely available in school canteens, local grocery stores, convenience stores, and supermarkets across Taiwan. The bread is inspired by US military rations and is made from a dough enriched with wheat germ and eggs. The name Apple Bread was chosen due to the popularity of Apple Sidra advertisements at the time and the status of apples as a rare and valuable fruit in the country in the 1960s."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-apple-bread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_bread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wheat germ","ingredient/wheat-germ"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cooking in Chinglish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookinginchinglish.com","note":"Taiwanese home recipes from Edwina, who grew up in Taiwan.","url":"https://cookinginchinglish.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tiny Urban Kitchen: Taiwanese Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tinyurbankitchen.com","note":"Small set of Taiwanese recipes from Jen Che, a Taiwanese American blogger in Hong Kong.","url":"https://tinyurbankitchen.com/taiwanese-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Taiwan cuisine","links":[["Ageh (food)","dish/ageh-food"],["Alivongvong","dish/alivongvong"],["Bah-uân","dish/bah-uan"],["Braised pork rice","dish/braised-pork-rice"],["Coffin bread","dish/coffin-bread"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Egg yolk pastry","dish/egg-yolk-pastry"],["Iron egg","dish/iron-egg"],["Kiâm-piánn","dish/kiam-piann"],["Ló͘-bah-pn̄g","dish/lo-bah-png"],["Mongolian barbecue","dish/mongolian-barbecue"],["Peanut ice cream roll","dish/peanut-ice-cream-roll"],["Penglai rice","dish/penglai-rice"],["Pineapple cake","dish/pineapple-cake"],["Pineapple shrimp balls","dish/pineapple-shrimp-balls"],["Pork ball","dish/pork-ball"],["Rechao","dish/rechao"],["Shuangbaotai","dish/shuangbaotai"],["Small sausage in large sausage","dish/small-sausage-in-large-sausage"],["Taiwanese beef noodle soup","dish/taiwanese-beef-noodle-soup"],["Taiwanese fried chicken","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken"],["Taiwanese fried chicken cutlet","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken-cutlet"],["Tàⁿ-á-mī","dish/ta-a-mi"],["Tea egg","dish/tea-egg"],["Ti-hueh-kué","dish/ti-hueh-kue"],["Tshik-á-mī","dish/tshik-a-mi"],["Tube rice pudding","dish/tube-rice-pudding"],["Xiaolongbao","dish/xiaolongbao"]]}]},"ingredient/tomatoes":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tomatoes","n":"Tomatoes","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 9 cuisines","intro":[["The tomato is a plant whose fruit is an edible berry that is eaten as a vegetable. The tomato is a member of the nightshade family that includes tobacco, potato, and chili peppers. It originated from western South America, and may have been domesticated there, in Mexico, or in Central America. The Spanish introduced tomatoes to Eurasia in the Columbian exchange in the 16th century."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tomatoes"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wi